  ```

- debug-build-asserts-on-source-after-external-cmdsubst: a build with
  `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug` (the only one where `assert()` is live)
  aborts in `fd_setfd()` when a script sources a file whose path came
  from a command substitution that ran an *external* command. A
  substitution running only builtins (`$(echo ...)`) is fine, so the
  fd left over from the forked child is what reaches `fd_setfd()`
  without `FD_READ`/`FD_WRITE` set. Every `tests/*.sh` file starts
  with exactly this pattern, so no test can run under a debug build.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  cmake -S . -B b -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug && cmake --build b -j
  echo 'echo sourced' > /tmp/s.sh
  printf 'D=$(dirname /tmp/x)\n. "$D/s.sh"\n' > /tmp/t.sh && ./b/shish /tmp/t.sh
  # shish: src/fd/fd_setfd.c:13: fd_setfd: Assertion `d->mode & FD_READWRITE' failed.
  ```

- exit-trap-loses-positional-parameters: an `EXIT` trap body sees `$#`
  as 0 and `$*` as empty, whatever the shell's positional parameters
  were. dash prints them unchanged.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  shish -c '( trap "echo \"\$#:\$*\"" EXIT; set -- a b )'   # 0:   (dash: 2:a b)
  ```

- syntax-error-in-c-string-exits-zero: a shell syntax error in a `-c`
  command string is reported but the shell still exits 0. POSIX requires
  a non-interactive shell to exit >0 on a syntax error (bash exits 2),
  and it does so for the same script in a file -- only the `-c` path
  loses the status.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  shish -c 'if [ 1 = 1 ]; then'; echo $?   # message, then 0 (bash: 2)
  echo 'if [ 1 = 1 ]; then' > s.sh
  shish s.sh; echo $?                      # message, then 1
  ```

- help-builtin-columns-overlap: `help` with no argument prints its
  two-column listing without a separator, so the left column's longer
  entries run straight into the right one
  (`hash [-lr] ... [name ...]type name ...`).

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  shish -c help | head -3
  ```

- mingw-getpid-implicit-declaration: `src/sh/sh_init.c:44` calls
  `getpid()` unconditionally, but `<unistd.h>` (where its declaration
  would come from) is only included `#if !WINDOWS_NATIVE`
  (`sh_init.c:12-16`), so a mingw cross build compiles it as an
  implicit `int`-returning declaration -- a warning, not a link
  failure (mingw's CRT exports a real `getpid()`, from `<process.h>`
  or its own `<unistd.h>`, either of which would fix the warning if
  included on this platform too). Found while fixing
  `mingw-missing-getppid`; not fixed here since it doesn't block the
  build.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  . ./cfg.sh && cfg-mingw64 -DDO_TESTS=OFF
  cmake --build build/x86_64-w64-mingw32
  # warning: implicit declaration of function 'getpid'
  ```

- mingw-missing-tcsetpgrp: `job_foreground.c:13` calls `tcsetpgrp()`
  to hand the controlling terminal to a job's process group; mingw has
  it and its siblings `tcgetpgrp()` (`job_init.c:43`) and `setpgid()`
  (`job_fork.c:126,133,187`, `job_wait.c:196`) nowhere, and Windows'
  console model has no controlling-terminal/foreground-process-group
  concept to map any of them onto. See `mingw-porting.md` section 5:
  the fix is compiling interactive job control out under
  `WINDOWS_NATIVE`, not shimming.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  . ./cfg.sh && cfg-mingw64 -DDO_TESTS=OFF
  cmake --build build/x86_64-w64-mingw32
  # undefined reference to `tcsetpgrp'
  ```

- no-tree-print-option-is-a-noop: `-DNO_TREE_PRINT=ON` changes nothing.
  `LIBSHELL_SOURCES` comes from `file(GLOB ...)` (`CMakeLists.txt:167`),
  so it holds absolute paths, and the two `list(REMOVE_ITEM ...)` calls
  at `CMakeLists.txt:198`-`199` pass relative ones -- they match nothing
  and silently do nothing. `-DNO_TREE_PRINT=1` reaches the compiler but
  no source tests it, so both `tree_print.c` and `tree_printlist.c` are
  still compiled and linked.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  cmake -S . -B /tmp/a -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=MinSizeRel
  cmake -S . -B /tmp/b -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=MinSizeRel -DNO_TREE_PRINT=ON
  cmake --build /tmp/a -j8; cmake --build /tmp/b -j8
  strip /tmp/a/shish /tmp/b/shish; stat -c%s /tmp/a/shish /tmp/b/shish
  # 189312 189312 -- byte-identical, and /tmp/b/.../tree_print.c.o exists
  ```
